WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.831
Which dye should you try first?

00:00:03.051 --> 00:00:13.459
Blink Atlas compares nine blinking HaloTag dyes. The curves show isolated detections expected per square micrometre per second in a fixed-cell experiment.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:21.888
JF630b emits more photons per millisecond. JF635b stays on longer.

00:00:22.108 --> 00:00:29.462
These are published measurements. The cropped images retain the original display intensities from the authors' recording.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:36.056
Change the assumed density. The broad range leaves several candidates.

00:00:36.276 --> 00:00:46.456
In this narrow, high-density example, two dyes stay in the lead across all the sensitivity checks. The range is an assumption you can change.

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:54.791
A higher photon threshold can leave too few candidates. A different sample protocol also withholds the ranking.

00:00:55.011 --> 00:00:59.463
You can export each analysis and import it again to reproduce the decision.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:08.737
The pilot recording comes from a separate live-cell experiment. The cyan rings mark the authors' fitted positions in the original camera frames.

00:01:08.957 --> 00:01:15.462
Here the exposure is one hundred milliseconds. This movie tests forecasting within one recording.

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:21.848
Only the first two hundred frames fit the forecasts. The next eight hundred are held out.

00:01:22.068 --> 00:01:32.466
The trend's average error was four hundred fifty-six detections per block. The constant baseline missed by seven hundred sixty-five. Both overestimated the final block.

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:38.905
In Methods and data, select a dye to trace its measurements to the original workbook.

00:01:39.125 --> 00:01:46.459
The tubulin benchmark also shows how clustering increases signal overlap compared with the uniform-density assumption.

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:52.245
To test whether the recommendation helps in the lab, start with existing matched recordings.

00:01:52.465 --> 00:01:59.474
The one-page proposal specifies equal pilot budgets and a comparison using localizations with uncertainty at or below twenty nanometres.
